James discussed a C++ function that uses a variable length array (VLA) for reversing a string, which triggered a compiler warning. He sought alternatives to avoid manual memory management. I suggested using `std::vector` for dynamic allocation to handle memory automatically without requiring deletion. I provided a code example demonstrating how to implement this while ensuring safety with type-casting. Additionally, I mentioned the option of using fixed-size arrays if a maximum size is known and cautioned against type-punning across incompatible types, recommending careful design for variable types.
